.TH DNS 4
.SH NAME
dns \- ask a resolver for a name's address
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dns
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dns
posts
.B /srv/dns
and serves one file,
.BR dns ,
which
.IR init (8)
mounts after the stack's tree at
.BR /net .
A program writes a name to
.B /net/dns
and reads the answer back on the same descriptor:
.IP
.EX
% echo fs.example.com > /net/dns
fs.example.com ip 10.0.0.2
.EE
.PP
The written line is a name, and a type after it that may only be
.BR ip .
A name the resolver does not know fails the write with
.BR ENOENT .
The answer belongs to the descriptor that asked, is read whole whatever
the offset, and is forgotten once read, the way
.IR cs (4)
answers.
.PP
The resolver asked is the one
.IR ipconfig (1)
learned, the
.B dns=
in
.BR /net/ndb ,
or failing that the
.B dns=
on the gateway's record in
.BR /lib/ndb/local .
The asking is one datagram each way on a conversation of
.BR /net/udp ,
waited on for two seconds and tried twice.
A name answered once is remembered, so
.IR cs (4),
which asks here for every dial of a name, does not ask the network twice.
.PP
A
.B Tremove
of the file stops the server.
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/dns/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cs (4),
.IR ipconfig (1),
.IR net (2)
.SH BUGS
Only addresses are asked for, and only the first in an answer is kept.
What is remembered is never forgotten, whatever time to live the resolver
gave it.
A resolver's answer is trusted as it stands.
